# 6. Image Quality Assessment

Image quality is a mandatory part of Phase 7.

A classification model cannot reliably assess a deck when the photograph is unclear.

The system must evaluate whether an image is suitable for analysis.

## 6.1 Image Quality Categories

### Good

The photograph has sufficient:

- Lighting.
- Focus.
- Visibility.
- Resolution.
- Deck surface coverage.

The model can proceed with condition analysis.

### Fair

The photograph is partially suitable.

Possible problems:

- Slight blur.
- Uneven lighting.
- Partial surface visibility.
- Minor obstruction.

The system may proceed with analysis if the visible portion is sufficient, but must communicate the limitation.

### Poor

The photograph is unsuitable for reliable analysis.

Possible problems:

- Heavy blur.
- Very low lighting.
- Excessive shadows.
- Very low resolution.
- Major obstruction.
- Deck surface not clearly visible.

Expected behavior:

- Do not produce an authoritative condition classification.
- Return an image-quality failure or unable-to-assess result.
- Ask the user to upload a better photograph.

## 6.2 Unable-to-Assess State

The system must support an explicit state:

`UNABLE_TO_ASSESS`

Use it when:

- The image is too poor.
- The deck surface is not identifiable.
- The model confidence is insufficient.
- The image is outside the supported analysis scope.
- The model cannot distinguish the visible condition reliably.

Never force a prediction simply because the API expects a condition level.

